.\" Title: vrunner-cat
.\" Author: vrunner contributors
.\" Manual section: 1
.\" Process this file with
.\"   groff -man -Tascii vrunner-cat.1

.TH VRUNNER-CAT 1 "2025" "vrunner" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
vrunner cat \- print the VTTY buffer of a running command

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vrunner cat
[\fITARGET\fR] [\fB\-\-color-always\fR]

.SH DESCRIPTION
Prints the virtual terminal (VTTY) buffer of a running command to stdout. By default, the output is plain text with all ANSI escape sequences stripped, making it safe for piping into \fBgrep\fR(1), \fBawk\fR(1), \fBless\fR(1), or redirecting to a file.

When no target is specified and exactly one command is running, that command is selected automatically. If multiple commands are running, a target must be specified either by PID (numeric) or by command name.

The output includes both the visible screen content and the scrollback history.

.SH OPTIONS

.TP
.BR \-\-color-always
Preserve ANSI color escape sequences in the output. When set, the buffer is rendered with its original colors, bold, underline, and other formatting exactly as the child process displayed it. Without this flag, all ANSI sequences are stripped and only plain text is printed. Useful for piping into \fBless \-R\fR or saving colored output.

.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help information and exit.

.SH TARGET SELECTION

If \fITARGET\fR is provided:
.RS
.IP \(bu 4
Numeric values are treated as a process ID (PID).
.IP \(bu 4
Non-numeric values are matched case-insensitively against command names.
.RE

If no \fITARGET\fR is provided and exactly one command is running, it is selected automatically. If multiple commands are running, an error is printed listing available commands.

.SH EXAMPLES

Print plain text output of the only running command:
.RS
.PP
\fB$ vrunner cat\fR
.RE

Print with ANSI colors preserved:
.RS
.PP
\fB$ vrunner cat \-\-color-always\fR
.RE

Target a specific command by PID:
.RS
.PP
\fB$ vrunner cat 12345\fR
.RE

Target by name with colors:
.RS
.PP
\fB$ vrunner cat \-\-color-always htop\fR
.RE

Save output to a file:
.RS
.PP
\fB$ vrunner cat my-server > output.log\fR
.RE

Search for a pattern in running command output:
.RS
.PP
\fB$ vrunner cat db-console | grep "ERROR"\fR
.RE

View colored output in a pager:
.RS
.PP
\fB$ vrunner cat \-\-color-always vim-session | less \-R\fR
.RE

.SH SEE ALSO
